; Provider-route executable family, 3 of 7 — the poisoned bucket, and
; the case with the most to say.
;
; A real provider refuses a source it cannot parse. The fixture makes
; that reachable from a source a human can read: a line spelling
; `!malformed` (nothing else about it is special) makes `lines` return a
; structured-error frame instead of names.
;
; So the member set for `lines/shader` cannot be computed — and the two
; references below cannot be *checked*, which is a different thing from
; being wrong. The bucket is poisoned: one `cross_ref_extraction_failed`
; at the source, and silence at every reference into it. The alternative
; — an empty member set — would report `not_cross_ref` twice here and
; N times in a real document, blaming the author for the provider's
; refusal and burying the one diagnostic that matters.
;
; That rule is what makes the feature safe for hosts that cannot run
; providers at all: see `cross-ref-provider-unavailable`, which reaches
; the same silence by the other door.
(use-plugin "lines")

(shader :name main :src "u_time\n!malformed")

(bind :uniform u_time)
(bind :uniform u_res)
